-- title:
--   Global candidate ordinals across levels
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Let level $k$ consume $\mathrm{count}(k)$ candidate ordinals and retain $\mathrm{size}(k)$ of them through order embeddings $e_k$.
--
--   1. **Base counter.** $B(0)=0$ and $B(k+1)=B(k)+\mathrm{count}(k)$, a monotone sequence.
--   2. **Labels.** The retained entry $v$ of level $k$ receives
--   $$
--   \mathrm{label}(v)=B(k)+e_k(v),
--   $$
--   which lies in $[B(k),B(k+1))$.
--   3. **Injectivity.** Labels at earlier levels precede later bases, and labels are injective across all levels, even though discarded candidates leave gaps.
